  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/ff6f5e2fbd530ee9d90c2adf02bb212886fb137c1d55c9e9d78958902ccc9f13.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ above. On which of the following intervals is the function continuous? \n\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,1]$\n- $x\\in [1,\\infty )$\n- $x\\in (-1,1)$\n\nYou may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram.",
  "worked_solution": "Let's examine each statement.\n\n- The function is *not* continuous on $(-\\infty ,1].$ Although the function is continuous on $(-\\infty ,1),$ it is not left-continuous at $x=1.$\n\n- The function is continuous on $[1,\\infty ).$ It is continuous on $(1,\\infty )$ and also right continuous at $x=1.$\n\n- The function is continuous on $(-1,1).$ It is continuous on $(-\\infty ,1)$ and this contains the interval $(-1,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
